=========================================================
ABSOLUTE UNITS (CSS FONT SIZE)
=========================================================

Definition:
Absolute units are fixed size units.
They do NOT depend on any other values.

This means:
- They do not depend on parent elements
- They do not depend on root font size
- They do not depend on screen size
- They do not scale automatically

Once an absolute value is set, the size stays the same
unless it is changed manually.

---------------------------------------------------------
TYPES OF ABSOLUTE UNITS IN CSS
---------------------------------------------------------

1. px   → pixels (most commonly used)
2. cm   → centimeters
3. mm   → millimeters
4. in   → inches
5. pt   → points
6. pc   → picas

Note:
In real-world web design, px is the only commonly used
absolute unit. Other units are rarely used because screen
measurements are not truly physical.

---------------------------------------------------------
PX (PIXELS) – DETAILED EXPLANATION
---------------------------------------------------------

What is a pixel?
A pixel (px) is the smallest visible unit on a screen.
It represents a single dot of display.

How px works:
- px gives an exact fixed size
- It does not change based on parent elements
- It does not change automatically for different devices

Meaning:
If you set a font size as 20px, the text will always be
20px unless you manually change it.

---------------------------------------------------------
DEFAULT BROWSER BEHAVIOR
---------------------------------------------------------

- Most browsers set the default font size to 16px
- Headings (h1, h2, etc.) appear larger because browsers
  apply predefined pixel values to them

---------------------------------------------------------
ADVANTAGES OF PX
---------------------------------------------------------

- Easy to understand
- Predictable and consistent
- Gives precise control
- Good for:
  * Borders
  * Icons
  * Small UI elements
  * Fixed spacing

---------------------------------------------------------
DISADVANTAGES OF PX
---------------------------------------------------------

- Not responsive by default
- Does not scale well across different screen sizes
- Not ideal for accessibility
- Requires many media queries for responsiveness

---------------------------------------------------------
WHEN TO USE PX
---------------------------------------------------------

Use px when:
- Exact size is required
- The element should not scale
- Working with small fixed components

Avoid px for:
- Body text
- Large layouts
- Responsive typography systems

---------------------------------------------------------
IMPORTANT NOTE
---------------------------------------------------------

Modern responsive design prefers relative units like
em and rem because they adapt automatically to different
screen sizes and user settings.
